{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975790,"nonce":"FCSBHehONmIYzXsbaKS+P2OjFSc=","title":"AOT shards each parse the whole bitcode stream","message":"Sharded emit lowers once, serializes to bitcode, and N threads each parse a copy\ninto their own context, keep a contiguous findex range, and strip the rest to\ndeclarations. Every shard still parses the whole stream before stripping.\n\nA slim stream was tried and retired: dropping 144,111 initializers kept 57MB of\n61, because the module is bodies, not data.\n\nWhat remains is either lazy materialization (no C API materializes a single\nfunction, so this needs a hand-declared entry point or a different reader), or\nlowering each shard separately from AIR so a whole-program module is never built.\nThe second is the only design that lowers peak memory rather than trading it\nagainst shard count.","files":null}]}